<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>lyrcore</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Lyric.Core</RootNamespace>
    <Description>Diagnostics, source management and the reading side of the .lyrbc format.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What BOTH sides need: diagnostics, the SourceManager, exit codes, CLI options — and the reading
    side of the bytecode format (Format, BytecodeModule, Reader, CodeDecoder, Disassembler,
    ModuleInfo) under Bytecode/.

    Reading the format lives here rather than with the VM because it is the shared contract and not
    a runtime property. 'lyrvm info' reads a module without executing it, 'lyric disasm' too, and
    the writing side needs the same op and tag constants. With the VM, 'lyrc' would drag the
    interpreter along.

    The namespaces stay apart ('Lyric.Core', 'Lyric.Bytecode'): an assembly is a shipping unit, a
    namespace is a concept.

    This assembly depends on NOTHING. An edge from here to Lyric.Frontend or Lyric.Vm breaks the
    split; tests/Lyric.Tests.Cli checks that mechanically.
  -->

</Project>
